{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"content_hash":"d80e9c07bc6a2cb567878f9dad87711e5371e4ab968b400358d6c060799281cc","digest":{"component_or_area":"T11 plan review rubric (LLM reviewer)","key_entities":["src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_T11.md","tests/unit/test_plan_review_t11_rubric.py","docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md","POST-ROLLBACK SCHEMA STATE","python -m rebar.llm.plan_review.registry"],"problem_keywords":["false positives","migration review","plan review rubric","post-rollback schema state","applicability rule","append-only store","backward compatibility"],"propositions":["T11's unconditional post-rollback schema state finding rule generates tautological low-priority advisories on append-only stores, obscuring genuine data-loss risks.","Post-rollback findings should apply only when a plan (a) changes interpretation of existing records, (b) rewrites/migrates records in place, (c) removes/narrows a read path, or (d) performs irreversible transformation.","Purely additive, backward-compatible persistence changes (new record types, optional fields, new tables with defaults) do not trigger post-rollback findings, as residual data is normal in append-only and backward-compatible systems.","The applicability rule names no specific store technology, making it portable across SQL, event stores, document databases, and config formats."]},"digest_hash_version":1,"model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8","schema":"ticket_digest_v1"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"TICKET_DIGEST","timestamp":1787490603711109001,"uuid":"23f8400a-ef0d-46e5-8f48-95bdf94440c3"}